eir stomach.\n\nA mine took their port array. The deck went dark, then red. Dr. Ilsa Venn was already moving, cable in teeth, splicing by feel. 'Talk to me,' Pilot Vera Quill said, and Dr. Ilsa Venn talked — numbers, jokes, anything to keep the fear small."}, {'n': 4, 'title': "The Traitor's Price", 'text': "The traitor showed themselves at the third waypoint: the signal officer, paid in promises by Pirate Queen Vex. They seized a star chart etched in crystal and ran for the escape pod with Pirate Queen Vex's ships closing in.\n\nPilot Vera Quill laughed for the first time in days. 'All this way,' they said, 'and we were carrying the lock, not the treasure.' Dr. Ilsa Venn was already calculating the wake-up sequence.\n\nPilot Vera Quill let them live, confined to quarters. 'Pirate Queen Vex trades in hostages,' Pilot Vera Quill told the crew. 'We trade in something better.' It was a gamble; the crew's eyes said they knew it."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Grid Awakens', 'text': "The duel was legend before it ended: two ships in the fire-light, neither yielding, until Pilot Vera Quill did the impossible — dove through the grid's own gate, which a star chart etched in crystal held open, and watched Pirate Queen Vex break against the shield they could not see.\n\nThe flagship came on anyway, and Pilot Vera Quill met it ship to ship above the burning sky. It was Pirate Queen Vex themselves on the viewscreen, offering one last deal. Pilot Vera Quill answered with the engines at full burn.\n\nSilence, then. The grid humming. the Kepler Drift safe below. Dr. Ilsa Venn put a hand on Pilot Vera Quill's shoulder and said what everyone was thinking: 'We live.'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Home Signal', 'text': "Dr. Ilsa Venn was offered an admiralty and laughed in the committee's face. 'I have a ship,' they said. It was the highest compliment the crew had ever heard.\n\nThe traitor's family was found and freed; the traitor wept and was forgiven, and signed on again — this time for real. Pilot Vera Quill's rule about second chances held, barely, and gloriously.\n\nAnd when the next distress call came — as it always does, out in the drift — the engines were already warm. There are always more skies. There is always the Kepler Drift, somewhere, needing its heroes."}]}

